.TH SH-FRAME 1
.SH NAME
frame \- shell script interface to frame(2)'s structures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load frame

.B id := ${frame new}
.br
.B frame
.I id
.B spec
.I line...
.br
.B frame
.I id
.B read
.I file
.br
.B frame
.I id
.B solve
.br
.B ${frame
.I id
.B displacement
.I node
.RB ( x | y | r )
.B }
.br
.B ${frame
.I id
.B reaction
.I node
.RB ( x | y | r )
.B }
.br
.B ${frame
.I id
.B force
.I n
.B }
.br
.B ${frame
.I id
.B member
.I n
.RB [ deflected
.IR scale ]
.B }
.br
.B ${frame
.I id
.BR equilibrium | nodes | nnodes | nmembers | heldrot | text
.B }
.br
.B frame del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Frame
makes
.IR frame (2)
usable from
.IR sh (1).
What
.B spec
accumulates and
.B read
loads is
.IR frame (2)'s
own notation, passed through untouched, and the structure is parsed the
first time anything needs it.
.PP
.B Displacement
and
.B reaction
take a node name and a direction.
.B Force
gives a member's axial force, then the shear and moment at each end, in
the member's own directions.
.B Equilibrium
returns the three residuals, which must be zero.
.B Heldrot
counts the rotations
.IR frame (2)
held because nothing resisted them.
.SS Drawing a structure
.B Member
is the one thing here that is not a thin wrapper.
It returns a member's two endpoints,
.I x1 y1 x2 y2,
and with
.B deflected
it returns them moved by the displacements times a scale.
.PP
A structure has no natural curve to plot \- it is a set of segments, not
a series \- so a drawing of one wants exactly that, one member at a
time, each in its own table with its own
.B line
layer.
The scale is there because a real structure's displacements are far too
small to see: the truss in
.B /lib/demo/trusslab.sh
moves about three millimetres under a tonne, on a span of eight metres.
.SH EXAMPLE
The textbook triangle:
.IP
.EX
load std
load frame
f := ${frame new}
frame $f spec 'node a 0 0
node b 8 0
node c 4 3
bar a b 200e9 0.01
bar a c 200e9 0.01
bar c b 200e9 0.01
pin a
roller b y
force c 0 -1000'
frame $f solve
echo ${frame $f reaction a y}	# 500
echo ${frame $f force 0}		# 666.67, the bottom chord in tension
echo ${frame $f force 1}		# -833.33, a diagonal in compression
echo ${frame $f equilibrium}	# 0 0 0
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/frame.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR sh-circuit (1),
.IR frame (2),
.IR frametest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A structure that is a mechanism, a member naming a node that is not
there, a query before
.BR solve :
each fails the command, the first two with the message
.IR frame (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Frame (2)
underneath it is checked by
.IR frametest (1);
what is added here is handle bookkeeping and the endpoint extraction,
exercised by the example above and by
.BR /lib/demo/trusslab.sh .
.PP
A structure cannot be changed once parsed; a new handle is needed, which
is what the demo does on every slider move.
.PP
.B Member
reports only the two end positions, so a beam that bends between its
ends is drawn as the straight line joining them.
For a truss that is exact, since a bar really is straight; for a frame
it is not.
